{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>My son, you have gone up.</b> From that with which I suspected you — that a tearing beast had torn Joseph and I said that an evil beast had devoured him — this is Judah, who is likened to a lion; you acquitted yourself and said (Genesis 37:26): ״מה בצע כי נהרוג את אחינו״ — 'What gain is there that we kill our brother?'" "<b>And he mentioned</b> 'The scepter shall not depart from Judah until Shiloh come,' which comes to allude to the impending future redemption from Egypt. For how is it possible that the exile should now begin with Jacob and his seed, and he would not mention the matter of the first, imminent redeemer, nor prophesy about him, nor give them tidings of him — and instead mention the distant future redemption through the latter redeemer at the end of days? Therefore he said 'until Shiloh come,' which is an allusion to משה (Moses). And the meaning of the verse is that the kingship belongs to Judah until the time of Moses, when the kingship and the governance will return to the house of Levi — and that is Moses our teacher, the first redeemer, who reigned over Israel, in the manner of what is written (Deuteronomy 33): ״ויהי בישורון מלך בהתאסף ראשי עם״ — 'And there was a king in Jeshurun, when the heads of the people were gathered.' For all Israel assembled to him, and concerning this it says ״ולו יקהת עמים״ — 'and to him shall the gathering of peoples be.' And Israel are called עמים ('peoples'), in the manner of what is written (Deuteronomy 33): ״אף חובב עמים״. Here you have the pericah explained — within the blessing of Judah son of Jacob, and within the near future redemption awaiting them." "<b>And the Ramban, of blessed memory, wrote</b> regarding the plain sense of this verse: 'The scepter shall not depart from Judah' — for Jacob bequeathed the kingship and the governance to Judah, and blessed him that the kingship should never depart from the tribe of Judah to any other of the tribes. And he promised that whenever the kingship should be among Israel, it would be only from the tribe of Judah. But the meaning of the verse is not that the kingship would never depart from Judah from the time it begins, for there is a full verse (Deuteronomy 28:36): ״יולך ה׳ אותך ואת מלכך אשר תקים עליך״ and the like, and today we have no king or princes. Rather, the verse comes to say that all the time the kingship belongs to Israel, Judah is worthy of it, and if their kingdom is annulled on account of sin, it shall return to Judah — and this is the promise of this verse. Therefore David, of blessed memory, said (I Chronicles 28:4): ״ויבחר ה׳ אלהי ישראל בי להיות מלך על ישראל לעולם כי ביהודה בחר לנגיד ובבית יהודה בית אבי ובבני אבי בי״. And it is written (II Chronicles 13:5): ״כי ה׳ אלהי ישראל נתן ממלכה לדוד על ישראל לעולם לו ולבניו ברית מלח״. And since the kingship was given only to the tribe of Judah, therefore the kings who were not of the tribe of Judah were punished, and their kingdom did not succeed. As in the case of Saul, the first king of Israel, who was killed, he and his sons, and his kingdom did not endure. And similarly the Hasmoneans, whose kingdom perished and who were punished measure for measure: instead of wanting to reign when the prerogative of kingship was not theirs, they became servants to their servants — for the Holy One, blessed be He, set their servants to rule over them, and they cut them down. And this is what the Sages, of blessed memory, said: 'Anyone who says, I come from the house of the Hasmoneans — is a slave.' And it is what the prophet said (Hosea 8:4): ״הם המליכו ולא ממני השירו ולא ידעתי״ — 'They have set up kings, but not from Me; they have made princes, and I knew it not.' And besides this argument that the Hasmoneans were not of royal stock, there is yet another argument: they had no business reigning, for they were priests and were commanded in the Torah (Numbers 18:7): ״תשמרו את כהנתכם לכל דבר המזבח ולמבית לפרכת״ — 'You shall keep your priesthood for every matter of the altar and for within the veil.' Since this is so, they were not worthy of seeking the kingship but only of serving the service of HaShem; and because of this they were punished with this punishment." "<b>And so</b> we say in the Yerushalmi: 'They do not anoint kings from among the priests.' Rabbi Judah said: (Deuteronomy 17) ״למען יאריך ימים על ממלכתו״ — what is written after it? ״לא יהיה לכהנים״ and so forth. End of quote." "<b>There are those who explained</b> 'The scepter shall not depart from Judah' — for we find no kingship belonging to Judah until the rise of David the king. <b>Therefore</b> the verse says: no scion of the children of Judah shall reach the scepter of kingship, nor shall any from between his feet reach the status of lawgiver — and this is from the root of (Exodus 3): ״אסורה נא ואראה״, (Ruth 4): ״סורה שבה פה״ — that is, 'to turn aside, to reach.' <b>Until Shiloh come.</b> Until the Tabernacle of Shiloh is destroyed — and this is from the root of ״כי בא השמש״ ('for the sun has set'). And so we find explicitly in Scripture that the kingship did not belong to Judah until Shiloh was destroyed, as David said (Psalms 78): ״ויטוש משכן שילה אהל שכן באדם וימאס באהל יוסף ובשבט אפרים לא בחר ויבחר את שבט יהודה את הר ציון אשר אהב ויבחר בדוד עבדו ויקחהו ממכלאות צאן״. For after Shiloh was destroyed, Samuel the prophet came and anointed David." "<b>And it is possible</b> to explain that this passage, according to its plain sense, speaks of the blessing of Judah son of Jacob, and alludes to the two Messiahs who are destined to come: King David and King Messiah." "<b>And in my opinion</b>, for this reason three verses were written in the blessing of Judah before he begins with the blessing of his land's produce, and in each one of them he mentioned Judah — something you will not find regarding the rest of the tribes, that the name of one of them should be mentioned multiple times. And the matter was that he mentioned him three times in order to allude to all three: to Judah son of Jacob, to King David, and to King Messiah. The first verse, ״יהודה אתה יודוך אחיך״ — 'Judah, you shall your brothers praise' — refers to Judah son of Jacob, whom he blessed with might and with vengeance against his enemies and with the exercise of dominion over all his brothers, even the sons of his father; therefore he needed to use the expression אתה ('you'). The second verse, ״גור אריה יהודה״ — 'a lion's whelp, Judah' — refers to King David; he called him by the name Judah because he is of the seed of Judah, and he used the word גור (whelp) because in the days of Saul he was a whelp, and after the death of Saul he ruled over all Israel and became a lion. And he said, ״מטרף בני עלית״ — 'from the prey, my son, you ascended' — that he was saved from the prey of Saul, who pursued him. ״כרע רבץ כאריה״ — 'he crouched, he lay down like a lion' — in the days of Solomon his son, every man under his vine and under his fig tree. The third, ״לא יסור שבט מיהודה״ — 'the scepter shall not depart from Judah' — refers to King Messiah, the final Redeemer. And this is what it says: ״עד כי יבא שילה״ — 'until Shiloh come' — that is, his son who comes forth from his seed. And the reason for the word שילה, that it did not say 'his son,' is that Jacob alluded to the Messiah by the name Shiloh, that is, his son who will be born from a woman's שליה (afterbirth/placenta), in the manner of all who are born, from the language of (Deuteronomy 28:57) ״ובשליתה היוצאת מבין רגליה״. And the addition of the letter ה in the word שילה alludes to the final ה in the Name, and she is the daughter of Abraham, and she is the one who married Solomon, and she is the very attribute of King Messiah. Concerning him the Prophet, peace be upon him, alluded with five expressions of praise, as is written (Isaiah 11): ״ונחה עליו רוח ה׳ רוח חכמה ובינה רוח עצה וגבורה רוח דעת ויראת ה׳ והריחו ביראת ה׳״ — 'And the spirit of YHWH shall rest upon him, the spirit of wisdom and understanding, the spirit of counsel and might, the spirit of knowledge and the fear of YHWH; and he shall be fragrant in the fear of YHWH.' For surely in the time of King Messiah there will be an abundance of prophecy and wisdom and knowledge like waters covering the sea. And the meaning of the verse will be that the kingdom shall not depart from Judah forever, once Shiloh comes." "<b>And in the Midrash:</b> שילה — שי לו, יובילו שי למורא. <b>And know</b> Onkelos's rendering: שילה — that the kingdom is his. <b>And my master the Rav, R. Solomon, of blessed memory, explained</b> that the word עד in this place means 'forever' (לעד), and therefore the accent falls on עד to indicate that it does not attach to כי יבא שילה. From this comes the Targum of Onkelos: עד עלמא — 'forever' — and his Targum of כי יבא שילה is דייתא משיחא — 'until the Messiah comes'; and one who translates 'until the Messiah comes' is mistaken. And the sense of the verse is that when the Messiah comes, the kingdom shall not depart from Judah — forever, for eternity. And this is like the language spoken in Daniel (Daniel 2): ״די לעלמין לא תתחבל״ — 'it shall not be destroyed forever.'" "<b>And you should reflect</b> that in his saying ״עד כי יבא שילה״ — 'until Shiloh come' — there is included the first redeemer and the last redeemer, who will be of the sons of Judah and of his seed, and then he will merit to enter the Holy Land and to shepherd the flock of the Place. And this is the statement of the Sages, of blessed memory: 'שר (he sang) is not written, but rather ישיר (he will sing) — from here is the resurrection of the dead from the Torah.'" "<b>You will find</b> in Eileh HaDevarim Rabbah on the verse ״פסל לך״: He said to him: the Holy One, blessed be He — 'By your life, just as you gave your soul for them in this world, so too in the World to Come, when I bring them Elijah, the two of you will come as one.' From where is this derived? As it is written (Nahum 1:3): ״ה׳ בסופה ובשערה דרכו״ — 'YHWH's way is in the whirlwind and in the storm.' בסופה — this is Moses, as it is written (Exodus 2:3): ״ותשם בסוף״ — 'and she placed [him] among the reeds.' ובשערה — this is Elijah, as it is written (II Kings 2:11): ״ויעל אליהו בסערה השמימה״ — 'and Elijah went up by a whirlwind to heaven' — end of quotation. And this is the word of the Prophet (Micah 7:15): ״כימי צאתך מארץ מצרים אראנו נפלאות״ — 'As in the days of your coming out of the land of Egypt, I will show him wonders.' אראך ('I will show you') is not written, but rather אראנו ('I will show him'), that is, to the one who saw at first, I will show him again. And therefore it immediately follows with ״אסרי לגפן עירה״ and so on — speaking in praise of the land, of the blessing of its fruits and the abundance of its produce. And understand this." "<b>By way of the Midrash.</b> ״Your brothers shall praise you (יודוך אחיך)״ — since he mentioned to Reuben the matter of Bilhah, and to Simeon and Levi the matter of Shechem, Judah was afraid lest he mention to him the matter of Tamar. Therefore He said to him: ״Judah, you (יהודה אתה)״ — ״your brothers shall praise you (יודוך אחיך)״ — you acknowledged (הודית) the matter of Tamar; therefore ״your brothers shall praise you,״ to be for them a king in this world and in the world to come. In this world there arose from him thirty kings, as is said (Ruth 4:18): ״ואלה תולדות פרץ״ and so forth, and you count from David to Jehoiachin; and likewise in the world to come, as is said (Ezekiel 37): ״ודוד עבדי נשיא להם לעולם״. End quote." "<b>In Bereshit Rabbah.</b> ״My son, you have gone up (עלית)״ — Judah saved four: Joseph, Tamar, Perez, and Zerah; and the Holy One, blessed be He, saved from his seed four: Daniel, Hananiah, Mishael, and Azariah. End quote. The intent of this midrash is that corresponding to his saving Joseph from the pit, the Holy One, blessed be He, saved Daniel from the lions' pit; and corresponding to his saving Tamar and the two twins in her womb from the fire, so the Holy One, blessed be He, saved from his seed Hananiah, Mishael, and Azariah from the fiery furnace (גו אתון נורא יקידתא). And furthermore, the First Temple was built by Solomon, who was of the seed of Judah; the Second Temple was built by Zerubbabel son of Shealtiel, who was of the seed of Judah — as it is written (Haggai 2:23): ״נאם ה׳ צבאות אקחך זרובבל בן שאלתיאל עבדי״; and it is written (Zechariah 4:7): ״ידי זרובבל יסדו את הבית הזה״; and it is written (ibid.): ״מי אתה הר הגדול לפני זרובבל למישור״. The last Temple, which will be built speedily in our days, the King Messiah is destined to be of the seed of Judah, as is said (Isaiah 11:10): ״והיה ביום ההוא שרש ישי אשר עומד לנס עמים״." "<b>By way of the Kabbalah.</b> ״Judah, you — your brothers shall praise you (יהודה אתה יודוך אחיך)״. There is hinted in the name of Judah the beginning of the emanation/spreading (התחלת ההתפשטות), and this is the expression ״your brothers shall praise you (יודוך אחיך)״, for the name of all acknowledgment (הודאה) is between the sephirot (בין תבין). And from here the divine name of Gevurah (גבורה) went forth, for ״Judah prevailed (גבר) among his brothers,״ and this is alluded to in the words of Rebekah, and likewise at the beginning of the second blessing."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 49, :notes {0 "The translation preserves the reading by which Jacob addresses Judah, understanding מטרף בני עלית as 'my son, you have gone up' with the following words as an allusion to the suspicion about Joseph.", 10 "Hebrew citation יחזקאל לו normalized to Ezekiel 37, where the quoted verse appears; chapter 36 in Hebrew numbering would be chapter 36 in English as well, which does not contain this verse.", 12 "The phrase בין תבין (Aramaic: 'between the understandings/between the sephirot') is translated hyper-literally; the reference to 'the words of Rebekah' and 'the second blessing' are opaque allusions left untranslated per Kabbalah-passage protocol."}, :source-witnesses [{